    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To determine the number of different ways to have some of the library books and the rest checked out, we need to consider the possible numbers of books that can be checked out. Since there are six identical copies of the book, the possible numbers of checked-out books range from 1 to 5. This is because if no books were checked out, only one book could be in the library, and if all six books were checked out, only one book could be in the library. Therefore, there are 5 possible values for the number of checked-out books: 1, 2, 3, 4, and 5.\n\nFor each of these values, the number of ways to choose how many books to check out is simply the number of books checked out. Since the books are indistinguishable, the order in which they are checked out or not checked out does not matter. Thus, for each value of the number of checked-out books, there is exactly one way to determine how many books that is checked out.\n\nTherefore, the total number of different ways to have some books checked out and the rest in the library is the number of possible values for the number of checked-out books, which is 5.\n\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{5}\\).",
